.TH PNP 1 "2026-11-26" "pnp @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pnp \- one-time pad file encryption that stays secure even if P = NP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pnp
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR OUT ]
.RB [ \-\-key\-out
.IR PATH ]...
.RB [ \-\-shares
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-protect
.RB [ \-\-kdf\-memory
.IR MIB ]
.RB [ \-\-passphrase\-file
.IR FILE ]]
.I FILE
.br
.B pnp \-d
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR KEY ]...
.RB [ \-o
.IR OUT ]
.RB [ \-\-passphrase\-file
.IR FILE ]
.I FILE.pnp
.br
.B pnp \-\-verify
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR KEY ]...
.RB [ \-\-passphrase\-file
.IR FILE ]
.I FILE.pnp
.br
.B pnp
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.br
.B pnp \-\-sources
.br
.BR pnp " " \-\-gen\-passphrase [=\fIWORDS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pnp
encrypts any file with a one-time pad: a key of random bytes exactly as long as the file, combined with it by XOR.
Encrypting
.I FILE
writes the ciphertext
.I FILE.pnp
and the key
.IR FILE.pnp.key .
Decrypting
.I FILE.pnp
with
.I FILE.pnp.key
gives back
.IR FILE .
.PP
A one-time pad's security doesn't depend on any problem being hard to compute, so it holds even if P = NP, as long
as the key is truly random, kept secret, and used only once.
.PP
The key's bytes come from the machine's hardware random number generators:
.I /dev/hwrng
and the x86 CPU instruction RDSEED, each checked with the SP 800-90B start-up and continuous health tests. They are
combined with XOR, together with the kernel's
.BR getrandom (2).
If no hardware source is usable,
.B pnp
refuses to encrypt. Decrypting needs no random source.
.SS Interactive mode
.B pnp
alone, in a terminal, opens a file browser for the current folder. Arrow keys (or j and k) move, Space selects,
Enter opens a folder or goes on with the selection (or the highlighted file), Backspace goes up a folder, . shows
hidden files, c chooses the code cylinder, r looks again for drives and keys, and q quits.
.PP
For files to encrypt, it shows each output it will write before writing anything, and lets you choose where the
key goes (a drive, or beside the file), whether to split it into shares (each to its own drive), and whether to
lock it with a passphrase (typed, or made by
.BR \-\-gen\-passphrase ).
For .pnp files, it shows which key or shares it will decrypt with. A ciphertext is matched to its key by their
shared ID, so renamed files still match; a missing key or share is named and the file skipped.
.PP
The code cylinder (key drive) is looked for among the folders in
.I /media/$USER
and
.IR /run/media/$USER ,
preferring one named KEYS; keys are looked for at the top of each, and beside the ciphertext. When not in a
terminal, or with TERM=dumb,
.B pnp
alone prints its usage.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decrypt
Decrypt instead of encrypting.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Check
.I FILE.pnp
against its key or shares (found as for
.BR \-d ,
or given with
.BR \-k )
without writing anything: that they come from the same encryption, that their sizes match, and the integrity check
over every byte. Exit status 0 if the file is whole, 1 if not. For checking archived files now and then, or before
a real decrypt. A locked key
.RB ( \-\-protect )
needs its passphrase, as for decrypting.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIKEY\fR"
The key to decrypt with. Default:
.IR FILE.pnp.key ,
or if that doesn't exist, the shares
.IR FILE.pnp.key.1 ,
.IR FILE.pnp.key.2 ...
(share 1 says how many). For a key split with
.BR \-\-shares ,
give
.B \-k
once per share, in any order; all of them are needed.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIOUT\fR"
Where to write. Encrypting: the ciphertext (the key goes to
.IR OUT.key ).
Decrypting: the plaintext (needed when the ciphertext's name doesn't end in .pnp).
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-out " PATH"
Write the key straight into the folder
.I PATH
(under its usual name, such as
.IR FILE.pnp.key ),
or to the file name
.IR PATH ,
instead of beside the ciphertext. The key is built in that folder from the start, so it never touches the
ciphertext's disk: use it to write keys directly to removable media. With
.BR \-\-shares ,
give it once (a folder, for all shares) or once per share (share 1 to the first, and so on).
.TP
.BI \-\-shares " N"
Write the key as
.I N
shares (2 to 16) instead of one key file:
.IR FILE.pnp.key.1 " to " FILE.pnp.key.N .
Shares 1 to N\-1 are fresh pads from the hardware sources and share N is the pad XOR all of them, so any N\-1
shares reveal nothing about the key, and all N are needed to decrypt. Losing one share loses the file.
.TP
.B \-\-protect
Also lock the key (or each share) with a passphrase, asked for twice on the terminal. Decrypting sees that a key is
locked and asks for the passphrase. The passphrase goes through Balloon (BLAKE2b) and then Argon2id, two
memory-hard password hashes, and the result encrypts the key with ChaCha20, so every guess costs the
.B \-\-kdf\-memory
in memory and several seconds. This layer is only computationally secure: it slows down whoever steals a key file,
but it is not part of the P = NP guarantee. Forgetting the passphrase loses the file. See NOTES.
.TP
.BI \-\-kdf\-memory " MIB"
With
.BR \-\-protect :
the Argon2id memory in MiB (default 1024, 8 to 65536; Balloon gets 1/64 of it, at most 16 MiB). The decrypting
computer needs this much free memory: pnp checks MemAvailable in
.I /proc/meminfo
before hashing and stops if there's too little. Argon2id runs one lane per CPU (at most 64), in parallel threads;
a computer with fewer cores decrypts more slowly, with the same result.
.TP
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " FILE"
Read the passphrase from the first line of
.I FILE
(without its line ending) instead of asking on the terminal. For encrypting, it goes with
.BR \-\-protect .
.TP
.BR \-\-gen\-passphrase [=\fIWORDS\fR]
Print a passphrase of 7 words (or
.IR WORDS ,
5 to 20) from the EFF large word list (7776 words, about 12.9 bits each), chosen with the same hardware sources as
a pad, then exit. 7 words is about 90 bits, far beyond guessing at the cost
.B \-\-protect
puts on each guess; 10 is about 128 bits, as strong as a 128-bit key; 20 is about 256 bits, as strong as the
ChaCha20 key the lock ends in, which is why it stops there: more words would add nothing. A passphrase you have to
write down is only as safe as where you keep it.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Replace output files that already exist. Without it,
.B pnp
never replaces a file.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Refuse to run if the buffers holding pad, file and passphrase bytes can't be locked in memory (kept out of swap),
instead of only warning. With
.BR \-\-protect ,
the passphrase hashes' memory (about
.I MIB
plus 16 MiB) must be lockable too, which usually means raising the limit
.RB ( "ulimit \-l" ,
or memlock in /etc/security/limits.conf). Also for the interactive mode:
.BR "pnp \-\-strict" .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Don't print which random sources made the key, what was written, notes, or the progress line.
.IP
Without it, on a terminal,
.B pnp
shows a progress line for each file (how far along, how fast, how long is left) and for the passphrase hashing of
.BR \-\-protect .
It is never written when standard error isn't a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print every random source and its state before encrypting, and what the sandbox could do (see SANDBOX).
.TP
.B \-\-sources
List the hardware random sources and whether they can be used, then exit (status 1 if none can).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/hwrng
The kernel's hardware random number generator. pnp's udev rule makes it readable by the
.B pnp
group. Join it with
.BR "sudo usermod \-aG pnp $USER" ,
then log out and back in.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-pnp-hwrng.rules
That rule.
.TP
.IR /media/$USER/* ", " /run/media/$USER/*
Mounted drives: the interactive mode's candidates for the code cylinder.
.SH NOTES
Outputs are written under hidden names
.RI ( .NAME.XXXXXX.pnp-part )
and renamed into place only when complete, so a failure or Ctrl-C never leaves a partial file under a real name. The
key is created readable by its owner only (mode 600), as is a decrypted file.
.PP
With
.BR \-f ,
the files being replaced are first renamed aside (to hidden
.IR .NAME.XXXXXX.pnp-old )
and deleted only once every new file is in place. If saving any new file fails, the old ones are put back, so an
old key is never lost. After a crash at that moment, the old files are still there under those names (see
RECOVERY).
.PP
The health tests catch a hardware source that fails (stuck, repeating, badly biased); they can't prove one is truly
random. In a virtual machine, which
.B pnp
detects and mentions, the host can read the machine's memory and decide what RDSEED and /dev/hwrng return. If
.B pnp
can't lock its buffers in memory (ulimit \-l too low), it says so: pad and file bytes could then reach swap. With
.B \-\-strict
it stops instead.
.PP
A one-time pad hides the contents but doesn't detect changes, so
.B pnp
adds a one-time MAC (Wegman\-Carter, mod 2^127\-1), keyed by 32 more random bytes from the hardware sources kept in
the key, with its tag at the end of the ciphertext. Like the pad, it can't be forged with any amount of computing
power. If the ciphertext or the key has been changed or damaged, decrypting fails and nothing is written. Files from
pnp 0.1.0\-alpha.2 or older have no MAC; they still decrypt, with a note saying so. The ciphertext and key also
record which encryption they belong to, so a wrong key is refused.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-protect ,
the key is first checked to belong to the ciphertext, then the passphrase is asked for; a wrong one is refused at
once. A strong passphrase matters more than the memory setting: a short or common one can still be guessed.
.B \-\-gen\-passphrase
makes a strong one. A key locked with
.B \-\-protect
can only be unlocked by pnp 0.1.0\-alpha.4 or newer.
.PP
The key is as large as the file. Losing the key means losing the file.
.PP
The weakest link is how the keys are physically stored. Nothing can recover a file from its ciphertext alone, so an
attacker will go after the key instead: anyone who gets a key file (and its passphrase, with
.BR \-\-protect )
can read the file. Keep keys offline, apart from the ciphertext, and out of cloud storage and email.
.SH RECOVERY
A run that is killed, crashes or loses power can leave hidden files beside its outputs, never a partial file under
a real name. The next run that writes into or reads from that folder lists them.
.TP
.I .NAME.XXXXXX.pnp\-part
An output that was never finished. Delete it (it may hold pad or plaintext bytes), and run
.B pnp
again if
.I NAME
is missing.
.TP
.I .NAME.XXXXXX.pnp\-old
An old
.I NAME
that
.B \-f
moved aside to replace. To undo the interrupted run, put back every
.I .pnp\-old
file it left, ciphertext and key (or shares) together, over whatever is under the real names, and delete the
parts. Or, if
.B pnp \-\-verify
says the ciphertext and key under the real names are whole, delete the old ones. Don't mix an old ciphertext with
a new key.
.PP
Encrypting saves the key (or every share) before the ciphertext, so a ciphertext under its real name always has
its whole key. The file being encrypted is never changed.
.B pnp
never deletes or renames leftovers itself. More in docs/recovery.md.
.SH SANDBOX
.B pnp
sets no_new_privs and, from its start, a seccomp filter: every network system call fails, and running a program,
tracing another process or using io_uring ends
.B pnp
at once. Once a command-line encrypt, decrypt or
.B \-\-verify
has all its files open, a second filter allows only the system calls the rest of the work makes, and Landlock
(Linux 5.13+) limits file access to the output folders: creating, renaming and removing files there, nothing
else. The interactive mode keeps only the first filter, as it goes on to open other files. Each part is used when
the kernel offers it;
.B \-v
says which were.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on failure, 2 on a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getrandom (2),
.BR udev (7),
.BR seccomp (2),
.BR landlock (7)
.PP
